#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8226456a-01f0-5577-ae2a-e36c51dc2d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159309} {:answer/id #uuid "97023f76-2165-5875-884b-00124cfc7eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159310} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6021b0-6cc8-5e87-80d1-4225584902b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159311} {:answer/id #uuid "32780e7c-aac0-5688-a09b-948445ba520b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159312} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0c73b3-1f00-5eb0-b4a8-0954c2dec4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97023f76-2165-5875-884b-00124cfc7eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159310} :answer-field/id #uuid "2914e011-6467-5eb9-b672-4230ceda87a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a22cb81e-00a5-5c3a-9983-3ce07b82cf44" :question/math-academy-id "q-137105" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $9a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 16 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}72 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 18\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}72 & -36 & 20 \\\\ -36 & 18 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}72 & -36 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{18}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{18}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n9a+\\frac{b}{c}=9\\cdot \\frac{5}{18}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af51b0b6-4ae7-5b6a-b151-7f8cb88fa186" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Normal Equation for a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d1f5fd-cafb-568d-9657-f9184338194c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266187} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebabaa4-b41e-5183-994d-28b7279d1eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266846} {:answer/id #uuid "d0905648-e8ad-5aec-9b17-ddbb11a74a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268008} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e29d98-6dfb-5724-a307-5fe87ac6c792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "145" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268034} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b05ccb-64dd-53d9-9246-34046d164ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6b05ccb-64dd-53d9-9246-34046d164ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268331} :answer-field/id #uuid "a68dbddb-6995-52c9-8221-481a957ef459" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47be74ee-5888-5dc1-a458-d8d8cd278e9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-137878" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 20 \\\\ 20 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 29\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {3}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}37 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}55 \\\\ 29\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=37+17+55=109.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af51b0b6-4ae7-5b6a-b151-7f8cb88fa186" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Normal Equation for a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "598cbe9b-f2eb-52d5-9e41-b48441ed64d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412641} {:answer/id #uuid "84e93a6b-0108-570f-8119-23d513088357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412643} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9cc8d3-0106-5ea8-86d6-66b4ea64a0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412644} {:answer/id #uuid "934431cc-66ef-56d1-a66f-d360d9139b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412645} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cbe89f-eecc-5c0e-b93a-5822e57acd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "598cbe9b-f2eb-52d5-9e41-b48441ed64d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412641} :answer-field/id #uuid "b982590b-c792-58f1-b493-14196b43933c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d91806b-81b0-566e-90e0-48dc8925cff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-137886" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 20 \\\\ 20 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 40\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+6=56.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b1479a-f401-5be2-8d54-998ace998ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443601} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb217f8-2e73-5566-a846-de9689a469fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443602} {:answer/id #uuid "95c93c03-292b-584d-aa3a-7dff77290397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443603} {:answer/id #uuid "bc30efa1-ce98-5d2b-b5f5-b28ca6d26bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443604} {:answer/id #uuid "df31033e-dd1d-5f18-81f3-78e9e429df55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eb217f8-2e73-5566-a846-de9689a469fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443602} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc00c000-8569-5a9b-b4f0-7026f8c3241d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "642db6cf-d41e-5a55-a66e-30cab01320f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-137924" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 16 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}18 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 72\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}27 \\\\ -54\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -36 & 27 \\\\ -36 & 72 & -54\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}18 & -36 & 27 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{2}{1}=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf25468f-d230-53f1-a94f-8f18037a8dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363400} {:answer/id #uuid "24438d18-0c67-56f4-8912-fe1ead369af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363401} {:answer/id #uuid "4bea5562-78d7-5083-8d55-27b52c42ffd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363402} {:answer/id #uuid "1245d519-bb23-5605-8a30-d545c6f84f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363403} {:answer/id #uuid "ca976c63-1784-5536-a5ee-be049617b3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24438d18-0c67-56f4-8912-fe1ead369af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363401} :answer-field/id #uuid "b10893fa-4f68-5f42-a725-ae358c74185c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2664fcbd-5b65-5a40-b14f-fb7042b6173a" :question/math-academy-id "q-158373" :question/problem "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {3}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 9 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}22 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 11\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & ={[\\begin{aligned}11 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 22\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11\\cdot 22-8\\cdot 8}[\\begin{aligned}11 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 22\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{178}[\\begin{aligned}178 \\\\ 178\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
